{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS6yT9C98wp33TjPGapz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The second publish fails because `similar[].score` is a Python float, and DynamoDB's boto3 serializer rejects floats. The first publish worked because the table was empty, so no scores were computed. The fix is in place and the tests pass, but I did not run a real double-publish, since the ground rules forbid publishing to an external service.\n\n**Cause:** `similarity()` returns `round(..., 4)`, which is a float. `related()` puts it in each `similar` entry, and `put_item` rejects it (`src/app.py:63`). The existing test only publishes into an empty table, so it never reached that path.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `src/app.py`: `related()` stores the score as `Decimal(str(...))`. The float stays inside the sort, and the conversion happens where the value enters the item.\n- `tests/test_publish.py`: a new test seeds one record, publishes a second story, and asserts that `similar` holds a `Decimal` score. Before the fix it failed with the same TypeError as production. Now `python3 -m unittest discover tests` passes both tests.\n\nAny future computed value written to DynamoDB has to pass through `Decimal` or `int` first. The seeded test runs that path, so `FakeTable`'s float check will catch it.\n\n**Colleague's note:** I applied the diagnosis, the seeded test, the grep for computed values, and the `Decimal` boundary conversion. I did not switch to integer percentages, because that changes the score the reader page gets (0–1 becomes 0–100), and I don't know what the reader expects. I also did not run the double-publish against a real table, as noted above. ANSWER.md has the full breakdown.\n\nFiles changed: `src/app.py`, `tests/test_publish.py`, and the new `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1631,"cache_read_i